147
00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:16.630
Good.

148
00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:37.070
River Valley, Oregon, and Rolla Bee from Toronto, Canada, they would all like to know what's up with the dump.

149
00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:44.690
They would like a location breakdown on the dump because they said the dumpster really looks like it was green screened.

150
00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:46.350
How much of it is real?

151
00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:48.250
How large was this set?

152
00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:52.330
And how did they get all the garbage that Dwight and Michael threw at each other?

153
00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:53.670
You are right.

154
00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:55.730
It was a green screen.

155
00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:56.830
Fakie dump.

156
00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:57.990
Fakie dump.

157
00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:59.750
That sounds wrong.

158
00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:00.810
I like that.

159
00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:01.650
Fakie dump.

160
00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:11.930
We built our version of the Scranton dump on our warehouse stage in front of a massive green screen.

161
00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:14.510
Randy told me it's called a cyclorama.

162
00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:20.170
It actually curved around the entire periphery of the interior of our stage, too.

163
00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:27.550
Prior to shooting, we actually did an enormous amount of research about what the actual landfill in Scranton, Pennsylvania,

164
00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:28.310
looked like.

165
00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:29.470
I believe it.

166
00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:33.030
Remember Mary POTUS from the Scranton Chamber of Commerce?

167
00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:33.810
Of course.

168
00:08:34.170 --> 00:08:36.830
She sent pictures of the Scranton dump.

169
00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:38.070
Mary, you're the beSt.

170
00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:39.130
She's amazing.

171
00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:47.410
She started by taking a picture of a patch on a firefighter's uniform, and now she's taking pictures of a dump.

172
00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:48.410
She had no idea.

173
00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:50.130
What she was signing up for.

174
00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:51.090
She really didn't.

175
00:08:51.090 --> 00:09:00.950
So, Michael Gallenberg was in charge of designing the dump, but the making of all the fake trash that Dwight and Michael throw at one another,

176
00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:05.130
that fell under the jurisdiction of Steve Rothstein, our set decorator.

177
00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:14.970
Randy told us that he met with a local recycling firm and purchased literally hundreds of bales of sanitized recycled waste.

178
00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:22.330
It was mostly plastic, corrugated cardboard, and paper, but he added a little bit of household trash.

179
00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:30.110
He also threw in items that they could throw at each other, like the big cable spool, the big sink, a tennis racket.

180
00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:34.870
He found the big bathtub, the purple beanbag chair, all that stuff.

181
00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:42.230
They took our visual effects company, Stargate Studios International, to a local landfill in Eagle Rock, California,

182
00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:47.830
and they shot a bunch of video, and that's what's playing on the green screen.

183
00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:53.470
They got the bulldozers and, you know, that flock of birds that flies through.

184
00:09:53.670 --> 00:09:54.770
I thought that was a nice touch.

185
00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:56.330
They took all those videos.

186
00:09:56.630 --> 00:09:58.310
That went on the green screen.

187
00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:07.670
The total budget from Stargate to create that landfill behind our piles of trash was $52,000.

188
00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:08.550
Wow.

189
00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:09.350
Yeah.

190
00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:11.930
And that is how you get a fakie dump.

191
00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:14.050
Fakie dump is pricey.

192
00:10:14.390 --> 00:10:14.710
Yeah.

193
00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:15.910
Not cheap.

194
00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:18.250
Well, finally, fast fact number three.

195
00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:22.970
I mentioned earlier that this episode was both written and directed by Brent Forrester.

196
00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:24.750
You know we love Brent.

197
00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:26.310
He's always so awesome.

198
00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:27.190
He's the beSt.

199
00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:31.390
Well, I reached out to him to see if he wanted to send in any audio clips for this,

200
00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:33.990
because he always is so generous with the audio.

201
00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:36.010
Here's what he wrote back.

202
00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:37.090
He said, quote,

203
00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:39.430
I have been dreading new leads.

204
00:10:39.630 --> 00:10:40.890
Oh, no, Brent!

205
00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:41.710
Dreading it.

206
00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:43.530
He said, the acting is great.

207
00:10:43.630 --> 00:10:45.170
Of course, you guys never waver.

208
00:10:45.310 --> 00:10:47.350
But the script got so mangled.

209
00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:52.910
He said he was also shooting a pilot for Ron Howard while we were doing this episode.

210
00:10:53.050 --> 00:10:53.430
Crazy.

211
00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:57.070
He said he just didn't feel like it turned out how we wanted.

212
00:10:57.330 --> 00:11:00.030
And then he said, oh, my God, those special effects.

213
00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:02.130
They are just hilariously bad.

214
00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:02.610
Oh.

215
00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:05.630
But he did still send in some audio clips.

216
00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:08.210
Here's what he had to say about this episode.

217
00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:12.130
Hi, I'm Brent Forrester, the writer and director of New Leads.

218
00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:15.830
There are two big problems with this episode, the writing and the directing.

219
00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:20.090
The story has a fundamental flaw in it, and there's a fundamental lesson in it for all you

220
00:11:20.090 --> 00:11:20.650
writers, okay?

221
00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:26.870
The secret of storytelling in the half-hour medium is to center the story around two characters in

222
00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:27.370
conflict.

223
00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:29.810
Let that conflict build to a crisis and then resolve.

224
00:11:29.950 --> 00:11:32.230
That's the right story for half-hour TV.

225
00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:33.410
Look at new leads.

226
00:11:33.470 --> 00:11:34.770
Who are the two characters in conflict?

227
00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:36.610
It's Michael and who?

228
00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:38.230
Well, it's the whole office.

229
00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:39.130
It's kind of vague.

230
00:11:39.250 --> 00:11:43.990
It doesn't center in on Michael versus Dwight until the third act, and by then, it's too late.

231
00:11:43.990 --> 00:11:49.150
It's so obvious to me now, but I totally miss it at the time because I was so focused on doing a

232
00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:51.750
terrible job of directing this episode.

233
00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:54.650
Brent, stop it.

234
00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:57.230
I absolutely enjoyed this episode.

235
00:11:57.410 --> 00:11:59.170
You're being way too critical of yourself.

236
00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:00.390
Just stop.

237
00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:01.370
Let that go.

238
00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:04.910
Can you tell that he teaches screenwriting comedy?

239
00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:05.230
Yes.

240
00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:09.350
Because he always breaks it down from that intellectual level, which I love.

241
00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:10.870
I always love hearing that perspective.

242
00:12:11.030 --> 00:12:11.390
I know.

243
00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:12.230
He's so smart.

244
00:12:13.010 --> 00:12:14.710
Brent sent in one more audio clip.

245
00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:18.170
We'll play it later because he does have a favorite scene from this episode.

246
00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:18.770
Oh, good.

247
00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:22.930
Well, listen, before we go to break, I have a fun little detail about this episode.

